      "Vol. II — Its pilgrims understand what the priests refuse: that to be eaten and remade is the oldest sacrament, older than hearths, older than tides. The Warren takes the sick, the spent, the finished — and returns them CHANGED, never the same, sometimes better. The Sun's 'victims' walk out with new eyes and are called lost. They are not lost. They are FOUND, the way a bone sets stronger at the break.\n\nOne woman entered with lungs of stone and emerged breathing the void. She spoke of a gentle swallowing, a dark warmth that erased her name and gave her a new, voiceless song. The Sun's inquisitors called her a demon; she called them ghosts.\n\nA smudge of grey ash obscures the text here. [ink washed out] A faint sketch of a humanoid figure with too many joints occupies the lower right corner, though the caption is gone.\n\nWe do not seek the cure the Pale Liturgy promises, for their saints are merely frozen. We seek the transmutation of the void, where the breaking of the body is the beginning of sight.",
